samtools 1.16.1 / bcftools 1.16 / tabix·htslib 1.16
  Debian bookworm packages: samtools 1.16.1-1, bcftools 1.16-1,
  tabix 1.16+ds-3, libhts3 1.16+ds-3
  License: MIT / Expat  (htslib's cram/ subdirectory is Modified-BSD-3)
  Copyright: Genome Research Ltd.; Heng Li; Broad Institute; Illumina, Inc.;
             Peter Cock / The James Hutton Institute (samtools)
  Upstream texts READ (2026-09), at the exact matching tags:
    https://github.com/samtools/samtools/blob/1.16.1/LICENSE
    https://github.com/samtools/bcftools/blob/1.16/LICENSE
    https://github.com/samtools/htslib/blob/1.16/LICENSE
  bcftools is dual MIT-or-GPL: its LICENSE states plainly that GSL support is
  "optional and disabled by default as explained in the INSTALL document".

  ⚠️ THERE IS A REAL TRAP HERE, AND IT IS NOT JUST THEORETICAL.
  Debian's own /usr/share/doc/bcftools/copyright states:

      "License: MIT or GPL-3.0
       Comment: Dual-licensing is in effect for the default compilation mode.
       When linked with the GPL-licensed GNU Scientific Library (as is done for
       this Debian package), the resulting program must be distributed under
       the GPL."

  and Debian's debian/rules for bcftools 1.16-1 really does configure with
      --with-htslib=system --enable-gsl --with-cblas=gslcblas

  Read literally, that says the bcftools in this image is GPL-governed, which
  would make the "no copyleft in the toolchain" claim false. We therefore
  MEASURED it instead of trusting either the text or the metadata. Four
  independent checks, all reproducible inside the image:

    1. Behavior (decisive). Upstream bcftools/Makefile says the GSL dependency
       comes solely from the `polysomy` command, compiled only with
       `make USE_GPL=1`. So USE_GPL is exactly the GSL switch, and its
       observable effect is whether `bcftools polysomy` exists.
       MEASURED: `bcftools polysomy` → "unrecognized command". No GSL build.
    2. Byte-level. No `gsl_*` / `libgsl*` / `libcblas*` byte sequences in
       bcftools, samtools, tabix, freebayes, or any of the 39 bcftools plugins.
       This also rules out STATIC linkage, which `ldd` alone cannot see.
    3. Dynamic. No NEEDED entry mentions libgsl/libcblas in any of the above.
    4. Filesystem. No libgsl*/libcblas* file exists anywhere in the image.

  CONCLUSION: Debian's `--enable-gsl` does not put GSL into this bcftools.
  The copyright comment above is stale/templated with respect to this build.
  The claim holds — but only because we tested it, not because the metadata
  said so.

  This is enforced at build time by scripts/check_no_gsl.sh, which runs all four
  checks and fails the build. It has been negative-tested (scripts/
  _negtest_no_gsl.sh): injecting a fake libgsl.so, a bcftools that answers
  `polysomy`, or `gsl_*` symbol bytes each makes it fail, and it passes again
  once reverted. Do not "simplify" it back to a bare `ldd | grep libgsl` —
  that version has three blind spots (static linking, plugins, and behavior).

